<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Patch>
  <!-- CASE 3: Sequence sibling expansion test.
       This single PatchOperationSequence touches both TC_SeqSibling and TC_SeqTarget.
       When TestMod_Change makes TC_SeqTarget dirty (by editing its own patch for that def),
       any recompute sub-doc must also include TC_SeqSibling — otherwise the first op here
       (targeting TC_SeqSibling) finds no match, PatchOperationSequence returns false, and
       the op targeting TC_SeqTarget never runs.

       This is the key sub-doc completeness test: a def (TC_SeqSibling) that is not itself
       dirty must be present in the sub-doc as "context" because it is co-patched in the
       same sequence as a dirty def (TC_SeqTarget).

       This file does NOT change between runs — it is an UNCHANGED mod that happens to
       bundle both defs. The change-vehicle is TestMod_Change. -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationSequence">
    <operations>
      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_SeqSibling"]</xpath>
        <value>
          <seqTag>from-static-seq</seqTag>
        </value>
      </li>
      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_SeqTarget"]</xpath>
        <value>
          <seqTag>from-static-seq</seqTag>
        </value>
      </li>
    </operations>
  </Operation>

  <!-- CASE 5: PatchOperationConditional branch flip.
       Checks for the presence of <conditionalTrigger> on TC_Conditional:
         Run A (TestMod_Change does NOT add conditionalTrigger): nomatch branch fires
                → conditionalNoMatchTag is added.
         Run B (TestMod_Change DOES add conditionalTrigger):     match branch fires
                → conditionalMatchTag is added instead.

       TC_Conditional is dirty in run B because TestMod_Change's patch file changed (its
       patch file now adds conditionalTrigger, making the def's final resolved state differ
       from the cached baseline). The conditional itself is what makes the post-patch
       difference observably different depending on the trigger field. -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_Conditional"]/conditionalTrigger</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_Conditional"]</xpath>
      <value>
        <conditionalMatchTag>yes</conditionalMatchTag>
      </value>
    </match>
    <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_Conditional"]</xpath>
      <value>
        <conditionalNoMatchTag>no</conditionalNoMatchTag>
      </value>
    </nomatch>
  </Operation>

  <!-- CASE 6: CROSS-DEF PatchOperationConditional — the recompute-fidelity gap.
       Unlike CASE 5 (whose test and effect are both on TC_Conditional, so a dirty-only sub-doc
       still contains everything it reads), this conditional's TEST and EFFECT are on DIFFERENT
       defs:
         TEST   — does TC_CrossProbe carry <probeFlag>?  (a different, never-dirty def)
         EFFECT — add <crossTag> to TC_CrossEffect.
       Full load / full rebuild: TC_CrossProbe is present → match branch → crossTag=probe-present.
       Incremental recompute: when the expect-recompute-gap mode dirties TC_CrossEffect, the sub-doc holds
       TC_CrossEffect but NOT TC_CrossProbe (it is neither dirty nor a sequence sibling, so
       SubDocExpander does not pull it in). The test xpath finds nothing → nomatch branch →
       crossTag=probe-absent. The recomputed def therefore DIVERGES from the full rebuild — a
       genuine cross-def fidelity gap that the current pipeline cannot detect or avoid.

       This file is the UNCHANGED static mod, so SubDocExpander's changed-mod container-op fallback
       does NOT fire; the recompute runs and produces the wrong value. The case is inert in every
       other mode because nothing dirties TC_CrossEffect there. -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_CrossProbe"]/probeFlag</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_CrossEffect"]</xpath>
      <value>
        <crossTag>probe-present</crossTag>
      </value>
    </match>
    <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_CrossEffect"]</xpath>
      <value>
        <crossTag>probe-absent</crossTag>
      </value>
    </nomatch>
  </Operation>

  <!-- CASE 7: NESTED PatchOperationConditional — RecomputeAllowlist.BranchParentId regression
       (issue #25 / PR #37).
       OUTER conditional's test is on TC_NestedTarget itself (its own defName — always true, so the
       match branch always fires) — same-def, in sub-doc once TC_NestedTarget is dirty.
       Its match branch is an INNER conditional whose test is cross-def:
         TEST   — does TC_NestedProbe carry <probeFlag>?  (a different, never-dirty def)
         EFFECT — add <nestedTag> to TC_NestedTarget (match: probe-present, nomatch: probe-absent).
       Full load / full rebuild: TC_NestedProbe is present → inner match → nestedTag=probe-present.
       Before the BranchParentId fix: when the expect-nested-conditional mode dirties
       TC_NestedTarget, the leaf op's id (".../match.match") resolved to the OUTER conditional's
       (same-def, in-sub-doc) read set instead of the INNER one's (cross-def, absent from the
       sub-doc) — wrongly ADMITTING the recompute. The sub-doc then lacks TC_NestedProbe, the inner
       test finds nothing, takes nomatch, and the recomputed def DIVERGES from the full rebuild
       (nestedTag=probe-absent instead of probe-present) — silently wrong, not just declined.
       After the fix, BranchParentId resolves the immediate (inner, cross-def) parent and the
       allowlist declines, forcing a full-rebuild fallback that preserves the correct value.

       This file is the UNCHANGED static mod, so SubDocExpander's changed-mod container-op fallback
       does NOT fire on its own — the case isolates the allowlist's nested-parent resolution. Inert
       in every other mode because nothing dirties TC_NestedTarget there. -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_NestedTarget"]</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationConditional">
      <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_NestedProbe"]/probeFlag</xpath>
      <match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_NestedTarget"]</xpath>
        <value>
          <nestedTag>probe-present</nestedTag>
        </value>
      </match>
      <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_NestedTarget"]</xpath>
        <value>
          <nestedTag>probe-absent</nestedTag>
        </value>
      </nomatch>
    </match>
  </Operation>

  <!-- CASE 8: PatchOperationConditional nested inside a PatchOperationSequence (issue #25 item 3).
       CASE 6 proved a TOP-LEVEL cross-def conditional's test edge is captured; CASE 7 proved a
       conditional nested inside ANOTHER conditional's branch (".match.match" id) is captured. This
       case proves the remaining shape issue #25 calls out by name: a conditional living inside a
       sequence's <operations> list (".operations[0].match" id) — the OTHER branch of
       ProvenanceRecorder.GetChildPatches' label scheme (".{field}[{i}]" vs ".{field}").

       Cross-def test/effect split, same shape as CASE 6:
         TEST   — does TC_SeqNestedProbe carry <probeFlag>?  (a different, never-dirty def)
         EFFECT — add <seqNestedTag> to TC_SeqNestedTarget (match: probe-present, nomatch: probe-absent).
       TestMod_Change dirties TC_SeqNestedTarget directly (Seed 2). This file is the UNCHANGED
       static mod, so SubDocExpander's changed-mod container-op fallback does NOT fire on its own —
       the case isolates whether capture recorded the sequence-nested conditional's own read set at
       all. Inert in every other mode because nothing dirties TC_SeqNestedTarget there. -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationSequence">
    <operations>
      <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_SeqNestedProbe"]/probeFlag</xpath>
        <match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_SeqNestedTarget"]</xpath>
          <value>
            <seqNestedTag>probe-present</seqNestedTag>
          </value>
        </match>
        <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_SeqNestedTarget"]</xpath>
          <value>
            <seqNestedTag>probe-absent</seqNestedTag>
          </value>
        </nomatch>
      </li>
    </operations>
  </Operation>

  <!-- CASE 9: PatchOperationConditional whose xpath-EXISTENCE test target is supplied by a THIRD,
       unrelated mod (docs/patch-operations-coverage.md's open "Open nuance" for
       PatchOperationConditional — the untested add/remove counterpart to CASES 6-8, which all used
       content-field flips within an unchanged mod set). TC_ThirdModProbe comes from TestMod_ThirdMod,
       distinct from BOTH this conditional's owner (TestMod_Static, unchanged) and TC_CondTarget's
       owner (TestMod_Defs, unchanged). TestMod_ThirdMod is present for Run A and REMOVED before
       Run B — a pure mod-list change, not a patch-file edit — so TC_ThirdModProbe's def node itself
       disappears from the patched doc between runs while nothing else here changes.

       Unlike CASES 6-8 (whose read set is a field WITHIN an existing, never-removed def), this test
       is on the EXISTENCE of the def node itself, driven purely by mod presence — the same shape as
       issue #40's PatchOperationFindMod gap, but for a conditional's node-existence test instead of
       an explicit mod-name check. Nothing dirties TC_CondTarget via Seed 1/2 (its own patch file
       never changes); this fixture's purpose is to reveal whether ANY existing mechanism (e.g. a
       MayRequire-style seed) already covers this case, or whether it's a genuine new gap. See
       expect-conditional-thirdmod. -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_ThirdModProbe"]</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_CondTarget"]</xpath>
      <value>
        <condThirdModTag>third-mod-present</condThirdModTag>
      </value>
    </match>
    <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_CondTarget"]</xpath>
      <value>
        <condThirdModTag>third-mod-absent</condThirdModTag>
      </value>
    </nomatch>
  </Operation>

  <!-- CASE 10: PatchOperationFindMod producing a dirty def -- live proof of the "unmodelled op
       kind" decline (RecomputeAllowlist's unknown-op-kind backlog: AddModExtension, EditResearch,
       AddIf/ReplaceIf/RemoveIf, FindMod -- see RecomputeAllowlistTests.FindMod_Declined for the
       offline pin of the same decision). Tests for "Harmony" (brrainz.harmony's display name),
       which is ALWAYS active in this harness, so <match> fires identically in both runs -- this
       fixture is not about FindMod's branch evaluation (TestMod_FindMod / #40 already covers
       that), only about whether an op producing a dirty def that isn't PatchOperationAdd/
       Replace/Remove/AttributeSet/AttributeAdd/AttributeRemove/SetName is safely declined rather
       than silently admitted. TestMod_Change dirties TC_OpKind_Target directly (Seed 2); this
       file is the UNCHANGED static mod, so SubDocExpander's changed-mod fallback does not fire on
       its own -- the case isolates the allowlist's op-kind check. Inert in every other mode
       because nothing dirties TC_OpKind_Target there. See expect-op-kind. -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationFindMod">
    <mods>
      <li>Harmony</li>
    </mods>
    <match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_OpKind_Target"]</xpath>
      <value>
        <opKindTag>findmod-matched</opKindTag>
      </value>
    </match>
  </Operation>

  <!-- CASE 11: same-mod, same-def op ordering -- positive regression for DefRecompute's documented
       "candidate bodies fed to patches are RAW" caveat (DefRecompute.cs). Op A (an unconditional
       Add) sets <orderFlag>on</orderFlag> on TC_Order_Source; Op B (a PatchOperationConditional
       immediately after it in this same file) tests THAT SAME def's <orderFlag> and, on match,
       adds <orderTag>flag-was-on</orderTag> to TC_Order_Source itself. Because both the test and
       the effect are on TC_Order_Source (same-def, allowlist-admitted once it is dirty),
       DefRecompute must apply Op A before Op B over the sub-doc -- exactly the order
       LoadedModManager.ApplyPatches uses -- for the conditional to see orderFlag=on and take the
       match branch, matching a full rebuild. TestMod_Change dirties TC_Order_Source directly
       (Seed 2). This is a POSITIVE proof that intra-mod op ordering is preserved for the common
       (both-defs-in-sub-doc) case; it does NOT reproduce the harder cross-mod raw-body staleness
       DefRecompute's comment warns about (a def mutated by mod X's patch but pulled into the
       sub-doc only as unpatched context for mod Y's read) -- that scenario needs a live
       investigation before it can be built into a deterministic fixture. See
       expect-order-preserved. -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationAdd">
    <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_Order_Source"]</xpath>
    <value>
      <orderFlag>on</orderFlag>
    </value>
  </Operation>
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_Order_Source"]/orderFlag[.="on"]</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="TC_Order_Source"]</xpath>
      <value>
        <orderTag>flag-was-on</orderTag>
      </value>
    </match>
  </Operation>
</Patch>
